think Designed the decisive crux before coding and hand-traced it: a solid 5x5 block's 16-pixel ring reduces at tolerance 0.5 to EXACTLY its four corners (each edge perfectly collinear, so any tolerance below the shape's extent keeps only the corners), an L to MORE THAN FOUR at the same tolerance, and a huge tolerance to the two-vertex floor (each chain collapses to its two anchors).
think Bounded the recursion by unrolling Douglas-Peucker onto an explicit ring-sized work stack -- no unbounded call depth (TAME: no unbounded recursion), the stack guarding PolygonTooComplex by name; kept-vertex set sized to the ring length, itself bounded by the pixel ceiling.
think Closed-ring adaptation: anchor at the start and the farthest point (unique argmax by strict > for determinism), split into two open chains, walk each; collect kept real indices in ring order so the polygon comes out an ordered subset starting at the ring start.
